--TEST--
`Core\Test::request` runs a request through the compiled route table in this process, so the program answering it reads the match the door took — `rule:testing/in-process-request`, `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 18's first mechanism: no socket and no port, and the table it matches
// against is the one this file's own `#[Route]` declarations compiled to. What
// is asserted is that the *match* crossed -- the child reads
// `Core\Request::route()` and finds the row of the method below, which is the whole of
// "dispatches through the compiled route table". Nothing here dispatches: what
// a match means is the program's (`rule:routing/the-servers-match-dispatches-nothing`), and the program is the
// `if` branch below.
//
// **The branch is the request.** The program answering an in-process request
// is this same program -- its script frame is what a served request runs --
// and `Core\Env::context()` is `Request` in that run
// (`rule:core-classes/env-context`), so one file holds both sides.
class Api {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/users/1", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(): string { return "shown"; }
}

if (Core\Env::context() == Core\Env::Context::Request) {
    // The child. `route()` is the match the caller took before this isolate
    // existed, which is `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`'s "once, before any application code
    // runs" holding for a synthetic request exactly as it does for an arrived
    // one.
    var $matched = Core\Request::route();
    if ($matched == null) {
        echo "the table claimed nothing";
    } else {
        echo $matched->is(Api::show) ? "Api::show" : "another route";
    }
} else {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/users/1");
    echo "status ", $answered->status(), "\n";
    echo "body ", $answered->body(), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
status 200
body Api::show
